{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790524197,"nonce":"oQvXpEGaA9pdPt+KqGHfLXF3bK0=","target":"347e1e6c563784940ba6cd87291035c92f83142a163905ec2827ed1b7587badb","message":"spectral_norm, branch perf/jit-spectral-norm (cb35221..0bc35c2, on 2bd8f3f). Cloud box, x86_64, 4 cores, LLVM 21.1.8, LuaJIT 2.1 (git20231223). Interleaved, best of 10 (10-round kernel) / best of 5 (100-round copy):\n\n| build | 10 rounds | 100 rounds |\n|---|---|---|\n| llvm, 2bd8f3f | 65 ms | 352 ms |\n| llvm, 0bc35c2 | 45 ms | 202 ms |\n| cranelift only, 2bd8f3f | 63 ms | 557 ms |\n| cranelift only, 0bc35c2 | 37 ms | 342 ms |\n| luajit | 11.7 ms | 142 ms |\n\nRatio on the shipped (llvm) build here: 5.6x -\u003e 3.9x. The 10-round run still finishes before the LLVM tier installs (multiplyAtv codegen ~40 ms), so it measures the Cranelift optimized tier.\n\nWhat each commit fixed:\n\n1. 91c9d27 (inner bound). infer_loop_carried_nums did not count ListCount as a Num (the main TypeSpecialize walk did), so `n = v.count` carried through the outer header dropped to val and the inner loop kept j as f64 behind a generic boxed compare. With it the inner loop is icmp_i64 and j is i64. This is the \"inner bound reaches the loop as a boxed block param\" item from #3; no param removal needed.\n2. 3c12742 (the loop-carried spill). In Cranelift, sum (f64) was stored to and reloaded from the stack every iteration, because the list read's slow path calls wren_subscript_get and every xmm register is caller-saved; the loop-carried add went through store forwarding. A SubscriptGet whose result is followed by GuardNumAt now leaves through a deopt at the read (call_pc + live + call_live, the same state the LLVM backend already used for guarded calls) instead of calling the helper. Sum now stays in xmm0. Done for LLVM too (miss_exit extended to SubscriptGet). One deopt marks the function speculation-failed, so a non-List receiver at such a site costs one recompile, not a loop.\n3. 7670519. Safepoint polls went on every block with a predecessor of higher id; inlined blocks (bb12/15/16 in multiplyAv) jump back to lower ids, so the inner loop polled three times per iteration. Headers now come from RPO (Cranelift and LLVM).\n4. 464efb2. x / 2^k lowers to x * 2^-k in Cranelift (exact, same rounding). Also covers an i64 constant converted, which is what IntSpecialize leaves for `/ 2`.\n5. da04827. The element read tested TypedArray before List.\n6. 0bc35c2 (bimodal runs, pre-existing). About 1 run in 10 of the 100-round copy took 2.7-3.3 s instead of ~0.4 s (7/40 under CPU load; also seen on 2bd8f3f). Cause: installing any tier of `denominator` called invalidate_ic_entries_for, which cleared every caller entry naming it, including kind 2 (closure, no code pointer). If multiplyAv's optimized compile snapshotted its ICs in that window, the denominator site read empty, the loop was planted cold, and since compiled code never refills ICs (JIT callsite IC population is off by default), recompile_top_tier found it still cold and gave up for good: the body stayed on generic calls. Now only entries holding an entry point (kinds 1/6) are downgraded to kind 2 with jit_ptr null; 2 and 7 stay; others clear as before. 0/40 stuck under the same load afterwards.\n   Separate weakness left in place: recompile_top_tier's still_cold path gives up permanently; any other way a site ends up empty after the interpreter stops running the function would recreate this.\n\nRemaining per-iteration cost in the Cranelift inner loop (~40 instructions vs LuaJIT's divider-bound loop):\n- List receiver checks (tag, type byte, count, elements load) on the loop-invariant `v` every iteration.\n- `object_is this` and i -\u003e f64 conversion every iteration: `i` and `this` are pass-through params of the inner header, so LICM cannot see them as invariant. Removing such params touches OSR entry layouts (loop headers are OSR targets), which is presumably why the earlier attempt was delicate.\n- One remaining spill/reload (`this`, [rsp+0x330]).\n- `.floor` (roundsd) that the Lua version does not have.\nNext options: hoist the List checks for an invariant receiver whose loop does not write it; then the pass-through params with OSR entries rebuilt.\n\nOther kernels, llvm build, 2bd8f3f vs 0bc35c2, best of 5: all within noise (fasta 28/30, pidigits 252/255, reverse_complement 36/36, regex_redux 401/382, method_call 18.3/19.0, mandelbrot 29/29, fib 10.0/9.6, nbody 86/82, binary_trees 206/185, fannkuch 1123/989, delta_blue 92/102). k_nucleotide is bimodal on both (best 166/135, median 207/224 over 20 runs) and takes the same pre-existing deopt at pc=473 in both.\n\nAlso cb35221: a clippy needless_range_loop in promote_fields that current toolchains flag.\n\nChecks run: fmt; clippy -D warnings release+debug, --features llvm, --features aot, -p wlift_lsp; both wasm lib checks; test --lib; e2e with and without llvm; every bench/*.wren and bench/ash/*.wren same output default vs WLIFT_TIER1=cranelift and vs 2bd8f3f, both builds.","files":null}]}